{"text": "This section includes only information that changes how support should be provided. For Lucas, the pertinent information is: mental health: clinician-informed support; behaviour: escape/avoidance when anxiety rises; safety: supervised re-entry and designated calm space; and medical: none affecting school. These details belong in the profile because they affect access, safety, regulation, communication, or independence. They should be shared only with staff who need the information and handled respectfully.\n\nThe priority challenges are the barriers we have already described: transitions into school; public correction or surprise performance; uncertain multi-step tasks; and returning after escalation or absence. The EA’s role is not to diagnose, redesign the student’s program independently, or provide constant help. The EA works under teacher direction and follows the IEP, health and safety plans, behaviour-support plans, and specialist recommendations.\n\nFor Lucas, the most useful EA actions are to use the agreed prevention and response plan; avoid power struggles and public consequences; reinforce approach and recovery; and collect data on triggers, response, and successful supports. In practice, this means preparing access before the activity, watching for the student’s communication, using the least intrusive prompt that works, and fading support when the student can take over.\n\nAcross every activity, four priorities remain constant: dignity, safety, access, and growing independence. We can now use the student’s actual learning goals to decide whether the program is adapted or modified.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-R-05.mp3", "words": [{"text": "This", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.3875, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 4}, {"text": "section", "start": 0.4, "end": 0.8875, "start_char": 5, "end_char": 12}, {"text": "includes", "start": 0.9, "end": 1.475, "start_char": 13, "end_char": 21}, {"text": "only", "start": 1.4875, "end"